      explain: [
        'The engineering design process is a cycle: define the problem; research; specify criteria and constraints; brainstorm and choose a solution; build a prototype; test; analyse results; and redesign. Unlike a single experiment, it is iterative — failures in testing are information that guides the next version.',
        'Criteria are the measurable goals a design should meet (carry 200 N, cost under $15, weigh less than 0.5 kg). Constraints are hard limits that cannot be violated (only the supplied materials, a 2-hour build time, must span 60 cm). Engineers often face trade-offs: a stronger beam is heavier, and a lighter one may cost more.',
        'A weighted decision matrix makes trade-offs explicit. Assign each criterion a weight (weights summing to 1), rate each design on each criterion, and compute the weighted score Σ(weight × rating). The matrix does not make the decision for you, but it forces the team to agree on priorities before favourites are chosen.',
        'Models predict before you build. A physical model may be a scale prototype; a mathematical model is an equation such as the pendulum period or a beam’s deflection; a computational model runs those equations on a computer. Scaling matters: if lengths scale by a factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes (and weights) by k³, so a model that works small may fail when enlarged.',
      ],
      vocab: [['Engineering design process', 'Iterative cycle of defining, designing, building, testing, and improving a solution.'], ['Criteria', 'Measurable requirements that define a successful design.'], ['Constraints', 'Limits on a design that cannot be exceeded.'], ['Prototype', 'Early working version built to test a design.'], ['Trade-off', 'Giving up some of one quality to gain another.'], ['Decision matrix', 'Table that scores designs against weighted criteria.'], ['Scale model', 'Model whose lengths are a fixed fraction of the real object’s.']],
